  "worked_solution": "Recall that $x=a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y=f(x)$ if $f(x)\\to \\infty $ or $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {a}^{+}$ or $x\\to {a}^{-}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/e6d80874f9150371204f12b2e933085f9b2666ac77fce5430f9645e5af073a36.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nWe also see that as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$ And, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty .$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -{2}^{+}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{-}$\n\n- $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$\n\nThese give us the asymptotes $x=-2$ and $x=0.$",
